-- title:
--   The usual multivariate stochastic order (corrected: Borel test functions)
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be a random vector, taking values in $\mathbb{R}^n$, on a probability space $(\Omega,\mu)$, and let $Y$ be a random vector, taking values in $\mathbb{R}^n$, on a (possibly different) probability space $(\Omega',\nu)$. $X$ is said to be **smaller than $Y$ in the usual multivariate stochastic order**, written $X \le_{st} Y$, if
--
--   $$E[\varphi(X)] \le E[\varphi(Y)] \quad \text{for every increasing Borel function } \varphi:\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R} \text{ for which the two expectations exist,}$$
--
--   where "increasing" means with respect to the coordinatewise partial order on $\mathbb{R}^n$ (Shaked & Shanthikumar, Eq. (6.B.4)). Equivalently, $P\{X\in U\}\le P\{Y\in U\}$ for every Borel upper set $U\subseteq\mathbb{R}^n$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** This is the corrected (`_v2`) form of `StochasticOrders_Multivariate_MultivariateOrder`: the test functions $\varphi$ are now required to be Borel measurable (`Measurable φ`), which the book's "$E[\varphi(X)]$" tacitly requires since $\varphi(X)$ must be a random variable. For $n \ge 2$ an increasing function on $\mathbb{R}^n$ need not be Borel (e.g. the indicator of the upper set generated by a non-measurable antichain), and for such $\varphi$ the Bochner integral $\int \varphi\circ X\,d\mu$ is not a function of the law of $X$; the previous definition was therefore not a relation between distributions, and the reverse direction of Theorem 6.B.1 failed for it even for measurable $X$, $Y$. Random vectors are functions into `Fin n → ℝ` with Mathlib's coordinatewise `Pi` order, so `Monotone φ` is the book's "increasing"; integrability of $\varphi\circ X$, $\varphi\circ Y$ is part of the $\forall$, matching "for which the expectations exist".
-- source:
--   Shaked & Shanthikumar, Stochastic Orders, Springer 2007, p. 266, Eq. (6.B.4)

import Mathlib

namespace StochasticOrders.Multivariate

open MeasureTheory

variable {Ω Ω' : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] [MeasurableSpace Ω']

/-- The usual multivariate stochastic order `X ≤st Y` (Shaked & Shanthikumar, *Stochastic
Orders*, Springer 2007, p. 266, Eq. (6.B.4)): an `n`-dimensional random vector `X` on `(Ω, μ)` is
smaller than an `n`-dimensional random vector `Y` on a (possibly different) probability space
`(Ω', ν)` in the usual multivariate stochastic order if `E[φ(X)] ≤ E[φ(Y)]` for every increasing
function `φ : (Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ` (increasing with respect to the coordinatewise order on
`Fin n → ℝ`, i.e. Mathlib's `Pi` order) for which the two expectations exist.

Corrected version (`_v2`): the test functions `φ` are required to be Borel measurable, as the
book's "E[φ(X)]" tacitly requires (`φ(X)` must be a random variable). For `n ≥ 2` an increasing
function on `ℝⁿ` need not be Borel (the indicator of the upper set generated by a non-measurable
antichain is increasing), and for such `φ` the Bochner integral `∫ φ ∘ X` is not a function of the
law of `X`, so the previous definition was not a relation between distributions and the book's
Theorem 6.B.1 failed for it in both directions. -/
def MultivariateOrder {n : ℕ} (μ : Measure Ω) (ν : Measure Ω')
    (X : Ω → Fin n → ℝ) (Y : Ω' → Fin n → ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∀ φ : (Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ, Measurable φ → Monotone φ → Integrable (φ ∘ X) μ →
    Integrable (φ ∘ Y) ν → ∫ ω, φ (X ω) ∂μ ≤ ∫ ω, φ (Y ω) ∂ν

end StochasticOrders.Multivariate


